James sought guidance on writing effective qualifying exam (QE) questions for PhD students, specifically in the context of public policy. Arachne outlined that a QE question should assess independent scholarly thinking, emphasizing conceptual command, synthesis, critical evaluation, application, research design, and scholarly judgment. Examples were provided to illustrate effective prompts, highlighting must-dos such as defining a clear problem, ensuring appropriateness of scope, and avoiding narrow factual demands. Arachne suggested common forms, templates, and rubrics to develop strong questions that demand analytical skills while ensuring clarity and relevance to training goals, ultimately underscoring the importance of drawing from theoretical and empirical literatures in question design.
